contravariant-derivative-of-scalar-field.conceptual_definition:
The contravariant derivative of a scalar field $\phi$, denoted $\partial^
u \phi$, is defined by applying the inverse metric tensor $g^{
u\mu}$ to the covariant derivative (or partial derivative) $\partial_\mu \phi$. The full relationship is $\partial^
u \phi = g^{
u\mu} \partial_\mu \phi$. For a flat Minkowski spacetime with signature $(+,-,-,-)$, where $g^{
u\mu} = \beta^{
u\mu} = 	ext{diag}(1, -1, -1, -1)$, this operation changes the sign of the spatial components: $\partial^0 \phi = \partial_0 \phi$ and $\partial^i \phi = -\partial_i \phi$ for $i=1,2,3$.
